dary \\( |z| = \\frac{1}{2} \\), which is a closed path. This means that \\( f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( z \\) within the open unit disc.\n\nStep 6: Conclusion\nSo, we can conclude that \\( f \\) is a constant function, and specifically, it is the zero function \\( f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( z \\) within the open unit disc."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. If \\( f \\) is an analytic function defined on the open unit disc such that \\( f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( |z| = \\frac{1}{2} \\), does it necessarily imply that \\( f \\) is a constant function? Justify your answer.\nassistant\n", "response": "Step 1: Analyze the given information\nWe are given that \\( f \\) is an analytic function defined on the open unit disc, and \\( f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( |z| = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nStep 2: Consider the properties of analytic functions\nSince \\( f \\) is analytic, it has a Taylor series expansion around any point in its domain. In particular, we can choose a point \\( z_0 \\) with \\( |z_0| < \\frac{1}{2} \\) and consider the Taylor series expansion of \\( f(z) \\) around \\( z_0 \\):\n\n\\[ f(z) = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n (z - z_0)^n \\]\n\nStep 3: Use the given condition \\( f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( |z| = \\frac{1}{2} \\)\nSince \\( f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( |z| = \\frac{1}{2} \\), we can plug in \\( z = \\frac{1}{2}e^{i\\theta} \\) into the Taylor series expansion:\n\n\\[ f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}e^{i\\theta}\\right) = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n \\left(\\frac{1}{2}e^{i\\theta} - z_0\\right)^n \\]\n\nStep 4: Apply the uniqueness theorem for analytic functions\nAccording to the uniqueness theorem for analytic functions, if \\( f(z) \\) is analytic in a domain and \\( f(z) = 0 \\) on a set of points with an accumulation point within the domain, then \\( f(z) \\) must be identically zero in the entire domain.\n\nStep 5: Apply the uniqueness theorem to our situation\nIn our 