[2026-04-07 06:52:23] [rlvr_pipeline.py (574)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 77670] [{"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F}=\\langle 7x,2y,5z\\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F}=\\langle 7x,2y,5z\\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume, we can use the Divergence Theorem. The Divergence Theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS} = \\iiint_V \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F} \\,dV\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute the divergence of the vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\). The divergence of \\(\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 7x, 2y, 5z \\rangle\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(7x) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2y) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(5z)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each partial derivative, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(7x) = 7, \\quad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2y) = 2, \\quad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(5z) = 5\n\\]\n\nAdding these together, the divergence is:\n\n\\[\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F} = 7 + 2 + 5 = 14\n\\]\n\nNow, we apply the Divergence Theorem:\n\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS} = \\iiint_V \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F} \\,dV = \\iiint_V 14 \\,dV\n\\]\n\nSince the region \\(V\\) has a unit volume, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\iiint_V 14 \\,dV = 14 \\cdot \\text{Volume}(V) = 14 \\cdot 1 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the surface integral is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\