, "response": "To evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 7x, 2y, 5z \\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume, we can use the divergence theorem (also known as Gauss's theorem). The divergence theorem states that for a vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) and a closed surface \\(S\\) enclosing a volume \\(V\\),\n\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS} = \\iiint_V \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F} \\, dV.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute the divergence of the vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(7x) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2y) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(5z).\n\\]\n\nCalculating each partial derivative, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(7x) = 7, \\quad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2y) = 2, \\quad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(5z) = 5.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} = 7 + 2 + 5 = 14.\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the divergence theorem to the region \\(V\\) with unit volume:\n\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS} = \\iiint_V \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} \\, dV = \\iiint_V 14 \\, dV.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(V\\) has a unit volume, the triple integral of 14 over \\(V\\) is simply:\n\n\\[\n\\iiint_V 14 \\, dV = 14 \\iiint_V dV = 14 \\cdot \\text{Volume of } V.\n\\]\n\nGiven that the volume of \\(V\\) is 1 unit:\n\n\\[\n14 \\cdot 1 = 14.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F}=\\langle 7x,2y,5z\\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\c