 flour as the recipe calls for. Therefore, we need to divide \\(\\frac{19}{5}\\) by 2:\n\\[ \\frac{19}{5} \\div 2 = \\frac{19}{5} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{19 \\times 1}{5 \\times 2} = \\frac{19}{10} \\]\n\nThe improper fraction \\(\\frac{19}{10}\\) can be converted back into a mixed number:\n\\[ \\frac{19}{10} = 1\\frac{9}{10} \\]\n\nThus, Stacy will use \\(\\boxed{1\\frac{9}{10}}\\) cups of flour."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. A recipe calls for $3\\frac{4}{5}$ cups of flour. However, Stacy wants to use half as much flour as the recipe calls for. How many cups of flour will Stacy use? Express your answer as a mixed number.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how much flour Stacy will use, we need to calculate half of the amount of flour the recipe calls for. The recipe calls for \\(3\\frac{4}{5}\\) cups of flour. First, we convert the mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n\\[3\\frac{4}{5} = 3 + \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{15}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{19}{5}\\]\n\nNext, we find half of \\(\\frac{19}{5}\\):\n\n\\[\\frac{19}{5} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{19 \\times 1}{5 \\times 2} = \\frac{19}{10}\\]\n\nNow, we convert the improper fraction \\(\\frac{19}{10}\\) back to a mixed number. We do this by dividing 19 by 10, which gives us a quotient of 1 and a remainder of 9. Therefore, \\(\\frac{19}{10} = 1\\frac{9}{10}\\).\n\nThus, Stacy will use \\(\\boxed{1\\frac{9}{10}}\\) cups of flour."}]
