 and \\( F \\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\( AO \\) (which is the radius of the sphere passing through \\( A \\)).\n   - Because \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) are symmetric with respect to \\( AO \\), and since \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) are the intersections of \\( BC \\) and \\( BD \\) with the plane \\( \\pi \\), this implies that \\( BC \\) and \\( BD \\) are symmetric with respect to the plane \\( \\pi \\) passing through \\( A \\) and perpendicular to \\( AB \\).\n\n4. **Determine the positional relationship between \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\):**\n   - Since \\( BC \\) and \\( BD \\) are symmetric with respect to the plane \\( \\pi \\), and \\( C \\) and \\( D \\) are points on the surface of the sphere, the line segment \\( CD \\) must be perpendicular to \\( AB \\). This is because the plane \\( \\pi \\) is perpendicular to \\( AB \\), and the points \\( C \\) and \\( D \\) are symmetric with respect to the plane \\( \\pi \\).\n\nTherefore, the positional relationship between \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\) is that \\( AB \\) is perpendicular to \\( CD \\). The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) be the endpoints of a diameter of a sphere, and \\( \\pi \\) be a plane passing through point \\( A \\) and perpendicular to \\( AB \\). \\( C \\) and \\( D \\) are two points on the surface of the sphere different from \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). Extending \\( BC \\) and \\( BD \\), they intersect the plane \\( \\pi \\) at points \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) respectively. If points \\( A \\), \\( E \\), and \\( F \\) form an isosceles triangle, what is the positional relationship between \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\)?\n\n(A) \\( AB \\) is perpendicular to \\( CD \\)\n(B) \\( AB \\) is parallel to \\( CD \\)\n(C) \\( AB \\) is perpendicular to \\( CD \\) and they are skew lines\n(D) Cannot be determine