 \\infty} n \\left( e^{\\ln(x)/n} - 1 \\right). \\]\nUsing the Taylor series expansion for the exponential function, we have:\n\\[ e^{\\ln(x)/n} = 1 + \\frac{\\ln(x)}{n} + \\frac{(\\ln(x))^2}{2n^2} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{n^3}\\right). \\]\nSubstituting this into the expression for \\( f(x) \\), we get:\n\\[ f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} n \\left( 1 + \\frac{\\ln(x)}{n} + \\frac{(\\ln(x))^2}{2n^2} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{n^3}\\right) - 1 \\right) = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} n \\left( \\frac{\\ln(x)}{n} + \\frac{(\\ln(x))^2}{2n^2} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{n^3}\\right) \\right). \\]\nSimplifying the expression inside the limit, we get:\n\\[ f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left( \\ln(x) + \\frac{(\\ln(x))^2}{2n} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{n^2}\\right) \\right) = \\ln(x). \\]\nTherefore, the function \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\\[ f(x) = \\ln(x). \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( f(xy) \\):\n\\[ f(xy) = \\ln(xy) = \\ln(x) + \\ln(y) = f(x) + f(y). \\]\n\nThus, the correct option is:\n\\[ \\boxed{A} \\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Given the function \\( f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} n(x^{1/n} - 1) \\), determine the value of \\( f(xy) \\). Choose the correct option:\n\nA) \\( f(x) + f(y) \\)  \nB) \\( \\frac{f(x)}{f(y)} \\)  \nC) \\( f(x) - f(y) \\)  \nD) \\( f(x)f(y) \\)\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine the value of \\( f(xy) \\) given the function \\( f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} n(x^{1/n} - 1) \\), we start by analyzing the function \\( f(x) \\).\n\nFirst, consider the limit:\n\\[ f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} n(x^{1/n} - 1). \\]\nWe can rewrite \\( x^{1/n} \\) using the exponential function:\n\\[ x^{1/n} = e^{\\frac{\\ln x}{n}}. \\]\nSo, the expression inside the limit becomes:\n\\[ n(x^{1/n} - 1) = n(e^{\\frac{\\ln x}{n}} - 1). \\]\nUsing the Taylor series expansion for \\( e^t \\approx 1 + t \\) for small \\( t \\), we get:\n\\[ e^{\\frac{\\ln x}{n}} \\approx 1 + \\frac{\\ln x}{n}. \\]\nSubstituting this into the expression, we have:\n\\[ n(e^{\\frac{\\ln x}{n}} - 1) \\approx n\\left(1 + \\frac{\\ln x}{n} - 1\\right) = n \\cdot \\frac{\\ln x}{n} = \\ln x. \\]\nTherefore, the limit is:\n\\[ f(x) = \\ln x. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( f(xy) \\). Using the property of the natural logarithm, we know:\n\\[ \\ln(xy) = \\ln x + \\ln y. \\]\nThus,\n\\[ f(xy) = \\ln(xy) = \\ln x + \\ln y = f(x) + f(y). \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(xy) \\) is \\( f(x) + f(y) \\), and the correct option is:\n\\[ \\boxed{A} \\]"}]
