\\(A^{50}\\) plus 1, i.e., \\(1+1 = 2\\), \\(1+1 = 2\\), and \\(0+1 = 1\\). Therefore, the eigenvalues of \\(I + A^{50}\\) are \\(2\\), \\(2\\), and \\(1\\).\n\nThe determinant of a matrix is the product of its eigenvalues. Thus, we have:\n\\[\n\\det(I + A^{50}) = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 4.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let \\( A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times3} \\) be a matrix with eigenvalues \\(1\\), \\(-1\\), and \\(0\\). Calculate \\( \\det \\left(I + A^{50} \\right) \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find \\(\\det(I + A^{50})\\), we first need to understand the eigenvalues of the matrix \\(A\\). Given that the eigenvalues of \\(A\\) are \\(1\\), \\(-1\\), and \\(0\\), we can use the properties of eigenvalues to determine the eigenvalues of \\(A^{50}\\).\n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(A^{50}\\) are the 50th powers of the eigenvalues of \\(A\\). Therefore, the eigenvalues of \\(A^{50}\\) are:\n\\[1^{50} = 1,\\]\n\\[(-1)^{50} = 1,\\]\n\\[0^{50} = 0.\\]\nSo, the eigenvalues of \\(A^{50}\\) are \\(1\\), \\(1\\), and \\(0\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix \\(I + A^{50}\\). If \\(\\lambda\\) is an eigenvalue of \\(A^{50}\\), then \\(\\lambda + 1\\) is an eigenvalue of \\(I + A^{50}\\). Therefore, the eigenvalues of \\(I + A^{50}\\) are:\n\\[1 + 1 = 2,\\]\n\\[1 + 1 = 2,\\]\n\\[0 + 1 = 1.\\]\nSo, the eigenvalues of \\(I + A^{50}\\) are \\(2\\), \\(2\\), and \\(1\\).\n\nThe determinant of a matrix is the product of its eigenvalues. Therefore, the determinant of \\(I + A^{50}\\) is:\n\\[2 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 4.\\]\nThus, the value of \\(\\det(I + A^{50})\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."}]
