ts at least one \\( i \\) such that \\( f(x_i) \\neq g(x_i) \\) for \\( f, g \\in F_k[x] \\) with \\( f \\neq g \\). This means that \\( f \\) and \\( g \\) must differ in at least one coefficient.\n\n5. **Interpolation and Polynomial Difference**: If \\( f \\neq g \\), then the polynomial \\( h = f - g \\) is non-zero and has degree at most \\( k \\). The polynomial \\( h \\) vanishes at some \\( x_i \\) if and only if \\( f(x_i) = g(x_i) \\). Since \\( f \\) and \\( g \\) are distinct, \\( h \\) has a root at some \\( x_i \\), and thus \\( h(x_i) = 0 \\) for some \\( i \\). This implies that \\( f(x_i) = g(x_i) \\) for some \\( i \\).\n\n6. **Minimum Distance Calculation**: To find the minimum distance, we need to determine the maximum number of points where two distinct polynomials can agree. A non-zero polynomial of degree at most \\( k \\) can have at most \\( k \\) roots. Therefore, two distinct polynomials in \\( F_k[x] \\) can agree at most at \\( k \\) points. This means that the Hamming distance between any two distinct codewords is at least \\( n - k \\).\n\n7. **Conclusion**: The minimum distance of the code \\( C \\) is \\( n - k \\). This is because we can always find two distinct polynomials that agree at \\( k \\) points, making the Hamming distance \\( n - k \\).\n\nThus, the minimum distance of the code \\( C \\) is \\(\\boxed{n-k}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Consider a finite field \\( F \\) with cardinality \\( q \\). Let \\( F_k[x] \\) be the set of polynomials in \\( F[x] \\) with degree less than or equal to \\( k \\). Given distinct elements \\( x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\) in \\( F \\), define a code \\( C \\) as in the original question. Determine the minimum distance of this code.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine the minimum distance of the code \\( C \\), we first need to understand the structure of the code. The cod