(x_1), f(x_2), \\ldots, f(x_n)) \\mid f \\in F_k[x] \\}\n   \\]\n   where \\( F_k[x] \\) is the set of polynomials in \\( F[x] \\) with degree less than or equal to \\( k \\).\n\n2. **Determine the Codeword Length**:\n   Each codeword in \\( C \\) is a vector of length \\( n \\).\n\n3. **Calculate the Number of Codewords**:\n   The number of polynomials in \\( F_k[x] \\) is \\( q^{k+1} \\) because a polynomial of degree at most \\( k \\) has \\( k+1 \\) coefficients, each of which can take \\( q \\) different values.\n\n4. **Minimum Distance**:\n   The minimum distance \\( d \\) of a code is the smallest number of positions in which any two distinct codewords differ. We need to find the smallest \\( d \\) such that any two distinct codewords \\( (f(x_1), f(x_2), \\ldots, f(x_n)) \\) and \\( (g(x_1), g(x_2), \\ldots, g(x_n)) \\) differ in at least \\( d \\) positions.\n\n5. **Use the Properties of Polynomials**:\n   If \\( f \\) and \\( g \\) are distinct polynomials in \\( F_k[x] \\), then \\( h = f - g \\) is a polynomial of degree at most \\( k \\). The number of distinct roots of \\( h \\) is at most \\( k \\) (by the fundamental theorem of algebra in finite fields).\n\n6. **Count the Positions Where Codewords Differ**:\n   Since \\( h \\) has at most \\( k \\) roots, there are at least \\( n - k \\) positions where \\( f(x_i) \\neq g(x_i) \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\).\n\n7. **Conclusion**:\n   The minimum distance \\( d \\) is \\( n - k \\).\n\nTherefore, the minimum distance of the code \\( C \\) is \\(\\boxed{n - k}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Consider a finite field \\( F \\) with cardinality \\( q \\). Let \\( F_k[x] \\) be the set of polynomials in \\( F[x] \\) with degree less than or equal to \\( k \\). Given distinct elements \\( x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\) in \\( F \\), define a code \\( C \\) as in the original question. Determin