ximizes the quadratic function \\( -x^2 - 6x + 12 \\), we can use the vertex formula for a parabola given by \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\). The vertex form provides the maximum or minimum value of the quadratic function, depending on the sign of \\( a \\). For a parabola that opens downwards (where \\( a < 0 \\)), the vertex gives the maximum value.\n\nThe vertex \\( x \\)-coordinate for a quadratic function \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\) is given by:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{b}{2a}\n\\]\n\nIn the given function \\( -x^2 - 6x + 12 \\), we identify the coefficients as:\n\\[\na = -1, \\quad b = -6, \\quad c = 12\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) into the vertex formula:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{-6}{2(-1)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{6}{-2} = -3\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) that gives the maximum value for \\( -x^2 - 6x + 12 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-3}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. The six faces of a three-inch wooden cube are each painted red. The cube is then cut into one-inch cubes along the lines shown in the diagram. How many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces? [asy]\n\npair A,B,C,D,E,F,G;\n\npair a,c,d,f,g,i,j,l,m,o,p,r,s,u,v,x,b,h;\n\nA=(0.8,1);\nB=(0,1.2);\nC=(1.6,1.3);\nD=(0.8,0);\nE=B-(A-D);\nF=C-(A-D);\nG=B+(C-A);\n\ndraw(E--D--F--C--G--B--A--D);\ndraw(A--C); draw(E--B);\n\na=(1/3)*D+(2/3)*E;\nc=(2/3)*D+(1/3)*E;\np=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*B;\nr=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*B;\n\ndraw(a--p);\ndraw(c--r);\n\nv=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*E;\nx=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*E;\nb=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*D;\nh=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(v--b);\ndraw(x--h);\n\ns=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*A;\nu=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*A;\nd=(1/3)*F+(2/3)*D;\nf=(2/3)*F+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(s--d);\ndraw(f--u);\n\ng=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*F;\ni=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*F;\n\ndraw(i--h);\ndraw(g--b);\n\nm=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*G;\no=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*G;\n\ndraw(m--u);\ndraw(o--s);\n\nj=(1/3)*G+(2/3)*C;\nl=(2/3)*G+(1/3)*C;\n\ndraw(l--p);\ndraw(r--j);\n\n[/asy]\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces, we need to analyze the structure of the cube and the faces of the smaller cubes.\n\n1. **Identify the types of smaller cubes:**\n   - **Corner cubes:** These cubes have paint on three faces.\n   - **Edge cubes (excluding corners):** These cubes have paint on two faces.\n   - **Face cubes (excluding edges and corners):** These cubes have paint on one face.\n   - **Inner cubes:** These cubes have no paint on any faces.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of each type of cube:**\n   - **Corner cubes:** There are 8 corners in a cube, so there are 8 corner cubes.\n   - **Edge cubes (excluding corners):** A cube has 12 edges, and each edge has 1 cube in the middle (since the cubes on the ends are corners). Therefore, there are \\(12 \\times (3-2) = 12\\) edge cubes.\n   - **Face cubes (excluding edges and corners):** Each face of the cube has a 1x1 grid of face cubes in the middle (since the cubes on the edges and corners are already counted). There are 6 faces, so there are \\(6 \\times (3-2)^2 = 6\\) face cubes.\n   - **Inner cubes:** These are the cubes completely inside the cube, which form a 1x1x1 cube. There is only 1 inner cube.\n\n3. **Count the cubes with paint on at least two faces:**\n   - **Corner cubes:** 8 cubes\n   - **Edge cubes (excluding corners):** 12 cubes\n\nAdding these together, the number of one-inch cubes that have red paint on at least two faces is:\n\\[8 + 12 = 20\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{20}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. The six faces of a three-inch wooden cube are each painted red. The cube is then cut into one-inch cubes along the lines shown in the diagram. How many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces? [asy]\n\npair A,B,C,D,E,F,G;\n\npair a,c,d,f,g,i,j,l,m,o,p,r,s,u,v,x,b,h;\n\nA=(0.8,1);\nB=(0,1.2);\nC=(1.6,1.3);\nD=(0.8,0);\nE=B-(A-D);\nF=C-(A-D);\nG=B+(C-A);\n\ndraw(E--D--F--C--G--B--A--D);\ndraw(A--C); draw(E--B);\n\na=(1/3)*D+(2/3)*E;\nc=(2/3)*D+(1/3)*E;\np=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*B;\nr=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*B;\n\ndraw(a--p);\ndraw(c--r);\n\nv=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*E;\nx=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*E;\nb=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*D;\nh=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(v--b);\ndraw(x--h);\n\ns=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*A;\nu=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*A;\nd=(1/3)*F+(2/3)*D;\nf=(2/3)*F+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(s--d);\ndraw(f--u);\n\ng=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*F;\ni=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*F;\n\ndraw(i--h);\ndraw(g--b);\n\nm=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*G;\no=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*G;\n\ndraw(m--u);\ndraw(o--s);\n\nj=(1/3)*G+(2/3)*C;\nl=(2/3)*G+(1/3)*C;\n\ndraw(l--p);\ndraw(r--j);\n\n[/asy]\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces, we need to analyze the cube step by step.\n\n1. **Total Number of Cubes:**\n   The original cube is 3 inches on each side, so when it is cut into one-inch cubes, we get:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 27 \\text{ one-inch cubes}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cubes with Three Faces Painted:**\n   These cubes are located at the vertices of the original cube. Since a cube has 8 vertices, there are:\n   \\[\n   8 \\text{ cubes with three faces painted}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Cubes with Two Faces Painted:**\n   These cubes are located along the edges of the original cube, excluding the vertices. Each edge of the original cube is 3 inches long, so when cut into one-inch cubes, each edge will have:\n   \\[\n   3 - 2 = 1 \\text{ cube with two faces painted}\n   \\]\n   Since a cube has 12 edges, the total number of cubes with two faces painted is:\n   \\[\n   12 \\times 1 = 12 \\text{ cubes with two faces painted}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cubes with One Face Painted:**\n   These cubes are located on the faces of the original cube, excluding the edges. Each face of the original cube is a 3x3 grid of one-inch cubes, so each face has:\n   \\[\n   (3 - 2)^2 = 1^2 = 1 \\text{ cube with one face painted}\n   \\]\n   Since a cube has 6 faces, the total number of cubes with one face painted is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 1 = 6 \\text{ cubes with one face painted}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Cubes with No Faces Painted:**\n   These cubes are located in the interior of the original cube, which is a 1x1x1 cube. Therefore, there is:\n   \\[\n   1^3 = 1 \\text{ cube with no faces painted}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Verification:**\n   Summing up all the cubes, we get:\n   \\[\n   8 \\text{ (three faces painted)} + 12 \\text{ (two faces painted)} + 6 \\text{ (one face painted)} + 1 \\text{ (no faces painted)} = 27 \\text{ cubes}\n   \\]\n   This confirms our calculations are correct.\n\nFinally, the number of one-inch cubes that have red paint on at least two faces is:\n\\[\n8 \\text{ (three faces painted)} + 12 \\text{ (two faces painted)} = 20\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{20}\n\\]"}]
