d{}. You connect the points (0, 0) and (9, 6) with a line segment. Starting at (0, 0), you move $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the way along the segment. What is the sum of the coordinates of the point you land upon?\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the coordinates of the point that is \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the way along the line segment connecting the points \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((9, 6)\\), we can use the section formula. The section formula for a point dividing a line segment joining \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in the ratio \\(m:n\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{mx_2 + nx_1}{m+n}, \\frac{my_2 + ny_1}{m+n} \\right)\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the points are \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((9, 6)\\), and the ratio is \\(1:2\\) (since \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the way along the segment means the point divides the segment in the ratio 1:2). So, we have \\(x_1 = 0\\), \\(y_1 = 0\\), \\(x_2 = 9\\), \\(y_2 = 6\\), \\(m = 1\\), and \\(n = 2\\). Plugging these values into the section formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1 \\cdot 9 + 2 \\cdot 0}{1+2}, \\frac{1 \\cdot 6 + 2 \\cdot 0}{1+2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{9 + 0}{3}, \\frac{6 + 0}{3} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{9}{3}, \\frac{6}{3} \\right) = (3, 2)\n\\]\n\nThe coordinates of the point that is \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the way along the line segment are \\((3, 2)\\). The sum of these coordinates is:\n\n\\[\n3 + 2 = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the coordinates of the point you land upon is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the sum: \\( \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{\\log_{l} 100!} \\). Choose the correct answer from the following options:\n\nA) 0.01\nB) 0.1\nC) 1\nD) 10\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the sum \\(\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{\\log_{l} 100!}\\), we start by using the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that \\(\\log_{l} 100! = \\frac{\\log_{10} 100!}{\\log_{10} l}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the sum as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{\\log_{l} 100!} = \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{\\log_{10} l}{\\log_{10} 100!}\n\\]\n\nThis can be factored out as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\log_{10} 100!} \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log_{10} l\n\\]\n\nNext, we recognize that \\(\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log_{10} l\\) is the logarithm of the product of the integers from 2 to 100. That is:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log_{10} l = \\log_{10} (2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 100) = \\log_{10} \\left(\\frac{100!}{1}\\right) = \\log_{10} 100!\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\log_{10} 100!} \\cdot \\log_{10} 100! = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the sum: \\( \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{\\log_{l} 100!} \\). Choose the correct answer from the following options:\n\nA) 0.01\nB) 0.1\nC) 1\nD) 10\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the sum \\(\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{\\log_{l} 100!}\\), we start by using the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that \\(\\log_{l} 100! = \\frac{\\log 100!}{\\log l}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the sum as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{\\log_{l} 100!} = \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{\\log l}{\\log 100!}\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out \\(\\frac{1}{\\log 100!}\\) from the sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\frac{\\log l}{\\log 100!} = \\frac{1}{\\log 100!} \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log l\n\\]\n\nThe sum \\(\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log l\\) can be rewritten using the properties of logarithms as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log l = \\log (2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 100) = \\log \\left(\\frac{100!}{1}\\right) = \\log 100!\n\\]\n\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\log 100!} \\sum_{l=2}^{100} \\log l = \\frac{1}{\\log 100!} \\log 100! = 1\n\\]