sitively oriented unit circle centered at the origin.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the contour integral \\( \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz \\), where \\( C(0,1)^+ \\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin, we can use the Cauchy integral formula. The Cauchy integral formula states that for a function \\( f(z) \\) that is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour \\( C \\), and for any point \\( z_0 \\) inside \\( C \\),\n\n\\[\n\\int_C \\frac{f(z)}{z - z_0} \\, dz = 2\\pi i f(z_0).\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the function \\( f(z) = \\sin(z) \\) is entire (i.e., analytic everywhere in the complex plane), and the contour \\( C(0,1)^+ \\) is the unit circle centered at the origin. Since \\( \\sin(z) \\) is analytic inside and on the unit circle, we can directly use the fact that the integral of an entire function over a closed contour is zero.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz = 0.\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the contour integral \\( \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz \\), where \\( C(0,1)^+ \\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the contour integral \\(\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz\\), where \\(C(0,1)^+\\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin, we will use the Cauchy Integral Theorem. The Cauchy Integral Theorem states that if a function \\(f(z)\\) is analytic (holomorphic) inside and on a simple closed contour \\(C\\), then the integral of \\(f(z)\\) over \\(C\\) is zero.\n\nHere, the function \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\) is an entire function, meaning it is holomorphic (analytic) everywhere in the complex plane. Therefore, it is certainly holomorphic inside and on the unit circle \\(C(0,1)^+\\).\n\nSince \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\) is analytic on and inside the contour \\(C(0,1)^+\\), the Cauchy Integral Theorem tells us that:\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz = 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the contour integral is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the contour integral \\( \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz \\), where \\( C(0,1)^+ \\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the contour integral \\(\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz\\), where \\(C(0,1)^+\\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin, we will use the Cauchy integral theorem. The Cauchy integral theorem states that if a function \\(f(z)\\) is analytic (holomorphic) inside and on a simple closed contour \\(C\\), then the integral of \\(f(z)\\) over \\(C\\) is zero.\n\nFirst, we identify the function \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\). The sine function, \\(\\sin(z)\\), is an entire function, meaning it is analytic (holomorphic) everywhere in the complex plane. Therefore, \\(\\sin(z)\\) is analytic inside and on the unit circle \\(C(0,1)^+\\).\n\nSince \\(\\sin(z)\\) is analytic within and on the unit circle, by the Cauchy integral theorem, we have:\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the contour integral is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the contour integral \\( \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz \\), where \\( C(0,1)^+ \\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the contour integral \\(\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz\\), where \\(C(0,1)^+\\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin, we can use the Cauchy Integral Formula. The Cauchy Integral Formula states that for a function \\(f(z)\\) that is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour \\(C\\), and for any point \\(a\\) inside \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\n\\int_C \\frac{f(z)}{z-a} \\, dz = 2\\pi i f(a).\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the function \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\) is analytic everywhere in the complex plane, and the contour \\(C(0,1)^+\\) is the unit circle centered at the origin. Therefore, \\(\\sin(z)\\) is analytic inside and on the unit circle.\n\nTo apply the Cauchy Integral Formula, we can rewrite the integral as\n\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz = \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\frac{\\sin(z)}{z-0} \\, dz = \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\frac{\\sin(z)}{z} \\, dz.\n\\]\n\nHere, we can see that \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\) and \\(a = 0\\). According to the Cauchy Integral Formula,\n\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\frac{\\sin(z)}{z} \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\sin(0).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin(0) = 0\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\frac{\\sin(z)}{z} \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\cdot 0 = 0.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the contour integral is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the contour integral \\( \\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz \\), where \\( C(0,1)^+ \\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the contour integral \\(\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz\\), where \\(C(0,1)^+\\) is the positively oriented unit circle centered at the origin, we can use the Cauchy integral formula. The Cauchy integral formula states that for a function \\(f(z)\\) that is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour \\(C\\), and for any point \\(a\\) inside \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\n\\int_C \\frac{f(z)}{z-a} \\, dz = 2\\pi i f(a).\n\\]\n\nIn our case, the function \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\) is analytic everywhere in the complex plane, including inside and on the unit circle \\(C(0,1)^+\\). The function \\(\\sin(z)\\) can be written as \\(\\sin(z) = \\frac{e^{iz} - e^{-iz}}{2i}\\), but we don't need to expand it to use the Cauchy integral formula directly.\n\nAccording to the Cauchy integral formula, for \\(f(z) = \\sin(z)\\) and \\(a = 0\\),\n\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\sin(0).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin(0) = 0\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\int_{C(0,1)^+} \\sin(z) \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\cdot 0 = 0.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the contour integral is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Given the elliptic curve \\(y^2 = x^3 - 15x + 22\\) and a point \\(P = (-1,6)\\), find the point \\(2P\\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the point \\(2P\\) on the elliptic curve \\(y^2 = x^3 - 15x + 22\\) where \\(P = (-1, 6)\\), we need to use the formula for doubling a point on an elliptic curve. The formula for doubling a point \\(P = (x_1, y_1)\\) on the curve \\(y^2 = x^3 + ax + b\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n2P = \\left( \\lambda^2 - 2x_1, \\lambda(x_1 - \\lambda^2 + 2x_1) - y_1 \\right)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda = \\frac{3x_1^2 + a}{2y_1}\\) for the curve \\(y^2 = x^3 + ax + b\\). In our case, \\(a = -15\\) and \\(b = 22\\), and \\(P = (-1, 6)\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\lambda\\):\n\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{3(-1)^2 + (-15)}{2 \\cdot 6} = \\frac{3 - 15}{12} = \\frac{-12}{12} = -1\n\\]\n\nNext, we use \\(\\lambda\\) to find the coordinates of \\(2P\\):\n\n\\[\nx_2 = \\lambda^2 - 2x_1 = (-1)^2 - 2(-1) = 1 + 2 = 3\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny_2 = \\lambda(x_1 - \\lambda^2 + 2x_1) - y_1 = -1(-1 - (-1)^2 + 2(-1)) - 6 = -1(-1 - 1 - 2) - 6 = -1(-4) - 6 = 4 - 6 = -2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the point \\(2P\\) is \\((3, -2)\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, -2)}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Given the elliptic curve \\(y^2 = x^3 - 15x + 22\\) and a point \\(P = (-1,6)\\), find the point \\(2P\\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the point \\(2P\\) on the elliptic curve \\(y^2 = x^3 - 15x + 22\\) where \\(P = (-1, 6)\\), we need to use the formula for doubling a point on an elliptic curve. The formula for doubling a point \\(P = (x_1, y_1)\\) on the curve \\(y^2 = x^3 + ax + b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n2P = (x_3, y_3)\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx_3 = \\left(\\frac{3x_1^2 + a}{2y_1}\\right)^2 - 2x_1\n\\]\n\\[\ny_3 = \\frac{3x_1^2 + a}{2y_1}(x_1 - x_3) - y_1\n\\]\nHere, \\(a = -15\\) and \\(b = 22\\). For our specific point \\(P = (-1, 6)\\), we have \\(x_1 = -1\\) and \\(y_1 = 6\\). Plugging these values into the formulas, we first calculate \\(x_3\\):\n\\[\nx_3 = \\left(\\frac{3(-1)^2 - 15}{2 \\cdot 6}\\right)^2 - 2(-1) = \\left(\\frac{3 - 15}{12}\\right)^2 + 2 = \\left(\\frac{-12}{12}\\right)^2 + 2 = (-1)^2 + 2 = 1 + 2 = 3\n\\]\nNext, we calculate \\(y_3\\):\n\\[\ny_3 = \\frac{3(-1)^2 - 15}{2 \\cdot 6}((-1) - 3) - 6 = \\left(\\frac{3 - 15}{12}\\right)(-4) - 6 = \\left(\\frac{-12}{12}\\right)(-4) - 6 = (-1)(-4) - 6 = 4 - 6 = -2\n\\]\nTherefore, the point \\(2P\\) is \\((3, -2)\\). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, -2)}\n\\]"}]
