which is \\(-10\\), divide by 2 to get \\(-5\\), and then square it to get \\(25\\). For the \\(y\\) terms, we take half of the coefficient of \\(y\\), which is \\(6\\), divide by 2 to get \\(3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\). Adding and subtracting these squares inside the equation, we get:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = -34 + 25 + 9.\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we have:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = 0,\\]\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0,\\]\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3.\\]\n\nAdding \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) together, we get:\n\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2.\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy the equation $x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34$.  What is $x+y$?\nassistant\n", "response": "To solve the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\) for the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), we start by rearranging the terms to bring all variables to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34.\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms. Starting with the \\(x\\) terms, \\(x^2 - 10x\\), we add and subtract 25:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x = (x - 5)^2 - 25.\\]\n\nNow, for the \\(y\\) terms, \\(y^2 + 6y\\), we add and subtract 9:\n\n\\[y^2 + 6y = (y + 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares back into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 3)^2 - 9 = -34.\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 34 = -34.\\]\n\nAdding 34 to both sides of the equation, we obtain:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must individually be zero. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\q