[2026-04-07 10:06:17] [rlvr_pipeline.py (574)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 77670] [{"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. If matrices $A$ and $B$ have more rows than columns, and the matrix $[A,B]$ has full column rank, does it follow that the transpose $[A,B]^T$ also has full column rank? Answer 'yes' or 'no' and justify your answer.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine whether the transpose \\( [A, B]^T \\) has full column rank given that matrices \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) have more rows than columns and \\( [A, B] \\) has full column rank, we will proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Define the dimensions of the matrices:**\n   - Let \\( A \\) be an \\( m \\times n_1 \\) matrix.\n   - Let \\( B \\) be an \\( m \\times n_2 \\) matrix.\n   - Since \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) have more rows than columns, \\( m > \\max(n_1, n_2) \\).\n\n2. **Define the block matrix:**\n   - The matrix \\( [A, B] \\) is an \\( m \\times (n_1 + n_2) \\) matrix.\n   - The transpose of \\( [A, B] \\) is \\( [A, B]^T \\), which is an \\( (n_1 + n_2) \\times m \\) matrix.\n\n3. **Full column rank condition:**\n   - The matrix \\( [A, B] \\) has full column rank if the rank of \\( [A, B] \\) is \\( n_1 + n_2 \\).\n   - This means that the columns of \\( [A, B] \\) are linearly independent.\n\n4. **Transpose of a matrix:**\n   - The rank of a matrix is equal to the rank of its transpose. Therefore, the rank of \\( [A, B] \\) is equal to the rank of \\( [A, B]^T \\).\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since \\( [A, B] \\) has full column rank, its rank is \\( n_1 + n_2 \\).\n   - Consequently, the rank of \\( [A, B]^T \\) is also \\( n_1 + n_2 \\).\n\n6. **Full column rank of \\( [A, B]^T \\):**\n   - An \\( (n_1 + n_2) \\times m \\) matrix has full column rank if its rank is \\( n_1 + n_2 \\).\n   - Since \\( m > n_1 + n_2 \\), an \\( (n_1 + n_2) \\times m \\) matrix with rank \\( n_1 + n_2 \\) has full column rank.\n\nTherefore, the transpose \\( [A, B]^T \\) also has full column rank.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{yes}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. If matrices $A$ and $B$ have more rows than columns, and the matrix $[A,B]$ has full column rank, does it follow that the transpose $[A,B]^T$ also has full column rank? Answer 'yes' or 'no' and justify your answer.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine whether the transpose of the matrix \\([A, B]^T\\) has full column rank given that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are matrices with more rows than columns and \\([A, B]\\) has full column rank, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the dimensions of the matrices:**\n   - Let \\(A\\) be an \\(m \\times n\\) matrix with \\(m > n\\).\n   - Let \\(B\\) be an \\(m \\times p\\) matrix with \\(m > p\\).\n   - The matrix \\([A, B]\\) is an \\(m \\times (n + p)\\) matrix.\n\n2. **Full column rank of \\([A, B]\\):**\n   - Since \\([A, B]\\) has full column rank, the rank of \\([A, B]\\) is \\(n + p\\).\n   - For an \\(m \\times (n + p)\\) matrix to have full column rank, \\(n + p \\leq m\\).\n\n3. **Transpose of \\([A, B]\\):**\n   - The transpose \\([A, B]^T\\) is a \\((n + p) \\times m\\) matrix.\n\n4. **Rank of the transpose \\([A, B]^T\\):**\n   - The rank of a matrix is equal to the rank of its transpose. Therefore, the rank of \\([A, B]^T\\) is also \\(n + p\\).\n\n5. **Full column rank of \\([A, B]^T\\):**\n   - For \\([A, B]^T\\) to have full column rank, its rank must be \\(n + p\\).\n   - Since \\([A, B]^T\\) is a \\((n + p) \\times m\\) matrix and its rank is \\(n + p\\) (given that \\(n + p \\leq m\\)), \\([A, B]^T\\) indeed has full column rank.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{yes}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. If matrices $A$ and $B$ have more rows than columns, and the matrix $[A,B]$ has full column rank, does it follow that the transpose $[A,B]^T$ also has full column rank? Answer 'yes' or 'no' and justify your answer.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine whether the transpose of the matrix \\([A, B]^T\\) has full column rank given that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have more rows than columns and \\([A, B]\\) has full column rank, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the dimensions and full column rank condition:**\n   - Let \\(A\\) be an \\(m \\times n\\) matrix and \\(B\\) be an \\(m \\times p\\) matrix.\n   - Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have more rows than columns, \\(m > n\\) and \\(m > p\\).\n   - The matrix \\([A, B]\\) is formed by concatenating \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) horizontally, resulting in an \\(m \\times (n + p)\\) matrix.\n   - Given that \\([A, B]\\) has full column rank, the rank of \\([A, B]\\) is \\(n + p\\).\n\n2. **Rank of \\([A, B]\\):**\n   - The rank of \\([A, B]\\) is the maximum number of linearly independent columns in \\([A, B]\\).\n   - Since \\([A, B]\\) has \\(n + p\\) columns and full column rank, it means all \\(n + p\\) columns are linearly independent.\n\n3. **Transpose of \\([A, B]\\):**\n   - The transpose of \\([A, B]\\) is \\([A, B]^T\\), which is an \\((n + p) \\times m\\) matrix.\n   - The rank of a matrix and its transpose are equal. Therefore, \\(\\text{rank}([A, B]^T) = \\text{rank}([A, B]) = n + p\\).\n\n4. **Full column rank of \\([A, B]^T\\):**\n   - For a matrix to have full column rank, the rank must be equal to the number of columns.\n   - The matrix \\([A, B]^T\\) has \\(n + p\\) columns.\n   - Since \\(\\text{rank}([A, B]^T) = n + p\\), \\([A, B]^T\\) has full column rank.\n\nTherefore, the transpose \\([A, B]^T\\) also has full column rank. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{yes}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within 